const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between a cornice and a valance?',
    a: 'A cornice is a rigid top treatment: a wooden board, padded, then wrapped in fabric so it reads as a clean architectural box. A valance is a soft fabric top treatment that gathers, pleats, or swags. Both sit at the top of the window and hide hardware. The cornice holds a straight line. The valance moves.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is a swag valance?',
    a: 'A swag is a valance style where the fabric is draped in loose curves across the top of the window, often with tails or jabots hanging at the sides. It is the formal, traditional version of a top treatment, common in dining rooms and living rooms that already have full drapery underneath.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can a cornice or valance go over existing blinds or shades?',
    a: 'Often, yes. A board-mounted cornice or valance can sit in front of an existing headrail as long as there is enough depth and the proportions work. We check that at the in-home consultation. If the existing treatment sits too far forward, we tell you before anyone builds anything.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How is a custom cornice built?',
    a: 'In the workroom. We cut a wood board to the measured width, add batting for a padded face, wrap the face fabric so the pattern lands where it should, finish the edges with welt or a contrast band, and build returns so the cornice meets the wall. Then it is hung on the same visit as any drapery it was designed with.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long does a custom cornice or valance take?',
    a: 'Most top treatments take three to five weeks from approved fabric to installation, depending on mill lead time and how detailed the design is. A simple tailored valance is faster than a multi-swag treatment with fringe and rosettes. We give a specific window at the estimate.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do cornices and valances work on bay windows and sliding doors?',
    a: 'Yes. Bay windows often get a cornice or a run of swags that follow the angles. Sliding glass doors get a wide board-mounted treatment or a swag-and-tail valance that clears the doorway when the panels are open. Those spans are why a workroom builds them rather than a store kit.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How much do custom cornices and valances cost?',
    a: 'Most custom top treatments in Pinellas County run $250 to $700 per window, depending on size, fabric, and detailing. A simple tailored valance sits at the lower end. An upholstered box cornice or a multi-swag treatment with trim sits higher. The quote is itemized after the consultation.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need drapery under a cornice or valance?',
